.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
.Dd April 22, 2026
.Dt KIT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kit
.Nd decentralized, content-addressed version control with cryptographic attribution
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl C Ar path
.Op Fl c Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
.Ar command
.Op Ar args ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a git-like command-line tool that produces signed, content-addressed
objects and exchanges them over pluggable transports
.Pq Cm kit+file , Cm kit+https , Cm kit+s3 , Cm kit+ssh .
Every commit is Ed25519-signed and identified by its BLAKE3 hash, so
chains of objects are self-verifying independent of the store they live
in.
.Pp
The on-disk layout under
.Pa .kit/
is deliberately similar to
.Pa .git/ :
objects are stored under
.Pa .kit/objects/ ,
refs under
.Pa .kit/refs/ ,
and the current branch is recorded in
.Pa .kit/HEAD .
A user-level config at
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kit/config
provides cross-repo defaults; repo-local values in
.Pa .kit/config
win over it for ordinary keys, but security-sensitive keys
.Pq Cm signing_key , Cm user.identity , Cm signer , Cm key.* , Cm attest.* , Cm ssh.* , Cm grant.webauthn_rp , No and Cm trusted_remote_endpoint
are user-scoped only and are ignored if set repo-locally.
.Sh GLOBAL OPTIONS
Parsed before
.Ar command
(so they apply to repository discovery), like git:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl C Ar path
Run as if
.Nm
were started in
.Ar path .
Repeatable; relative paths resolve against the prior
.Fl C .
.It Fl c Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
Set a config value for this invocation only. It is applied to the
effective config (never written to disk) and flows through the same
enforcement as a per-repo config file: security-sensitive keys are
refused, so
.Fl c
cannot spoof the signed author or redirect trust.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
The full list of subcommands is printed by
.Nm kit Fl Fl help .
The highlights:
.Bl -tag -width "cherry-pick"
.It Cm init Oo Fl q | Fl Fl quiet Oc Op Ar dir
Create a new
.Nm
repository in the current directory, or in
.Ar dir .
A missing
.Ar dir
is created, nested levels included. A
.Ar dir
that is a symlink or a file is an error.
.Fl q
drops the success line and never an error.
.It Cm add Oo Fl A | Fl Fl all Oc Oo Fl u | Fl Fl update Oc Oo Fl f Oc Oo Fl n | Fl Fl dry-run Oc Oo Fl p Oc Ar path ...
Stage files for the next commit. Respects
.Pa .gitignore
and
.Pa .kitignore
in each directory; deeper rules win. Patterns are relative to their file.
Per-directory rules override
.Pa .kit/info/exclude
and the global
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/ignore
(default
.Pa ~/.config/git/ignore ) .
An excluded directory cannot be re-included from below.
Multiple pathspecs may be given;
.Ql .
stages every non-ignored file under the current directory.
.Fl A
or
.Fl Fl all
stages every worktree change including deletions of tracked files (takes no
path arguments).
.Fl u
or
.Fl Fl update
restages only already-tracked files \(em updating modified ones and recording
deletions \(em without adding untracked paths (takes no path arguments).
.Fl A
and
.Fl u
are mutually exclusive.
.Fl f
or
.Fl Fl force
stages an explicitly-named ignored path (git refuses these without it;
already-tracked paths are never subject to ignore).
.Fl n
or
.Fl Fl dry-run
prints
.Ql add '<path>'
for each file that would be staged or updated, and
.Ql remove '<path>'
for each deletion that
.Fl A
would record. It changes no index and writes no object. It does not combine
with
.Fl p
or
.Fl u .
.Fl p
or
.Fl Fl patch
interactively presents each hunk between the staged (or HEAD) blob and the
worktree file, staging only the ones you accept: per hunk,
.Ql y
stages it,
.Ql n
skips it,
.Ql a
stages it and all later hunks in the file,
.Ql d
skips it and all later hunks, and
.Ql q
quits (keeping hunks already chosen). It requires explicit path arguments,
is incompatible with
.Fl A
and
.Fl u ,
and operates on regular text files only: a binary file is skipped with a
message (the command still succeeds), while symlinks and directories are
refused. An explicitly-named ignored path is refused unless
.Fl f
(like plain
.Cm add ) ,
and a path escaping the repository through a symlinked parent directory is
refused.
.It Cm rm Oo Fl Fl cached Oc Oo Fl r | Fl Fl recursive Oc Oo Fl f | Fl Fl force Oc Ar path ...
Remove paths and stage the deletion for the next commit. By default this
deletes the worktree file(s) and prunes now-empty parent directories.
Multiple pathspecs are accepted.
.Fl Fl cached
stages the removal only, keeping the worktree file(s).
.Fl r
or
.Fl Fl recursive
is required to remove a directory and everything under it.
.Fl f
or
.Fl Fl force
removes files even when their worktree content has diverged from the staged
blob; without it,
.Cm rm
refuses to destroy a locally-modified tracked file.
.It Cm mv Oo Fl f | Fl Fl force Oc Ar source ... Ar dest
Move or rename a tracked path and stage the change.
.Cm mv Ar src Ar dst
renames
.Ar src
to
.Ar dst ,
or moves it into
.Ar dst
when that is an existing directory; with more than one source,
.Ar dest
must be an existing directory. The worktree file is moved and the index
updated (source staged as removed, destination staged with the source's blob
and mode). kit has no rename detection, so
.Cm status
reports the move as a delete plus an add rather than git's
.Ql R .
.Fl f
or
.Fl Fl force
overwrites an existing destination; without it,
.Cm mv
refuses to clobber one. A missing or untracked source is refused.
A
.Ar source
may also be a directory:
.Cm mv Ar dir Ar newdir
renames it and
.Cm mv Ar dir Ar existing-dir
moves it inside, as
.Pa existing-dir/dir .
The directory is renamed in one filesystem operation, so untracked files
within it travel along (like
.Xr git 1 Ns 's
.Cm mv ) ,
and each tracked file beneath it is restaged at its new path. A directory
move refuses a pre-existing destination even with
.Fl f
(recursively removing it would strand tracked files and destroy untracked
ones), rejects overlapping sources
.Pq Cm mv Ar dir Ar dir/file Ar dest ,
and refuses moving a directory into itself.
.It Cm restore Oo Fl Fl staged Oc Oo Fl Fl worktree Oc Oo Fl Fl source Ar rev Oc Oo Fl f | Fl Fl force Oc Ar path ...
Restore path(s) in the worktree and/or unstage them. With no flag (or
.Fl Fl worktree ) ,
the worktree file(s) are rewritten from the staged index content,
discarding un-staged worktree edits.
.Fl Fl staged
instead reverts the index entry for each path to its
.Cm HEAD
version (unstaging it) while leaving the worktree file untouched;
.Fl Fl staged Fl Fl worktree
does both. A directory path restores every tracked entry at or below it.
.Fl Fl source Ar rev
takes the restored content from
.Ar rev Ns 's
tree (resolved as a branch, tag,
.Cm HEAD ,
full or short hash, or
.Cm HEAD~ Ns Ar n Ns / Ns Cm ^
navigation) instead of the index/HEAD default. The worktree restore
refuses to overwrite a file whose content diverges from the staged blob;
.Fl f
or
.Fl Fl force
discards that un-staged edit. A worktree restore never removes neighbouring
files \(em it only rewrites the named tracked paths.
.It Cm reset Oo Fl Fl soft | Fl Fl mixed | Fl Fl hard Oc Oo Fl f Oc Op Ar commit
Move
.Cm HEAD
.Pq and the current branch
to
.Ar commit
.Pq default Cm HEAD ,
resolved through the shared revision grammar (branch, tag,
.Cm HEAD ,
full or short hash,
.Cm HEAD~ Ns Ar n Ns / Ns Cm ^ ) .
.Fl Fl soft
moves
.Cm HEAD
only, leaving the index and worktree untouched.
.Fl Fl mixed
.Pq the default
also resets the index to the target commit's tree while leaving the
worktree.
.Fl Fl hard
additionally resets the worktree to the target tree \(em overwriting
tracked-file changes and removing tracked files the target drops, while
keeping untracked files (like git). As a safety divergence,
.Fl Fl hard
refuses to discard locally-modified or staged content unless
.Fl f
.Pq Fl Fl force
is given;
.Xr git 1 Cm reset Fl Fl hard
discards silently.
.It Cm clean Oo Fl n Oc Oo Fl f Oc Oo Fl d Oc Oo Fl x | Fl X Oc Op Ar path ...
Remove untracked files from the worktree. Destructive, so it refuses to
delete without
.Fl f
.Pq matching git's Cm clean.requireForce ;
.Fl n
previews with
.Ql Would remove <path>
lines. Without
.Fl d ,
untracked directories are left alone;
.Fl d
removes them too. Ignored files are kept unless
.Fl x
.Pq remove ignored too
or
.Fl X
.Pq remove only ignored .
Ignore matching uses kit's
.Pa .kitignore
matcher (basename/root-only subset).
.It Cm hash Ar file
Hash
.Ar file
as a blob, store it under
.Pa .kit/objects/ ,
and print the resulting BLAKE3 hash on stdout.
.It Cm cat Ar hash
Decode and print the object identified by
.Ar hash
on stdout.
.It Cm cat-file Bro Fl t | Fl s | Fl p Brc Ar object | Cm cat-file Fl Fl batch
Inspect a stored object (read-only plumbing, like
.Xr git 1 Cm cat-file ) .
.Fl t
prints the type
.Pq Cm blob Ns / Ns Cm tree Ns / Ns Cm commit Ns / Ns Cm tag Ns ; kit's Cm remix No is the one non-git type ,
.Fl s
the size (content byte length for blobs, kit's serialized size for
trees/commits), and
.Fl p
pretty-prints (blob bytes, a tree as
.Ql <mode> <type> <hash>\et<name>
lines, or a commit/tag summary).
.Fl Fl batch
reads object names from stdin (one per line) and emits a
.Ql <hash> <type> <size>
header plus the content for each
.Pq Ql <name> missing No for unknown objects .
.Ar object
resolves through the shared revspec grammar, or as
.Ar rev Ns : Ns Ar path ,
the object at
.Ar path
in the tree of
.Ar rev .
.It Cm show Oo Fl Fl stat Oc Op Ar object ...
Display objects, like
.Xr git 1 Cm show ,
defaulting to
.Cm HEAD .
An
.Ar object
may also be
.Ar rev Ns : Ns Ar path ,
the blob or tree at
.Ar path
in the tree of
.Ar rev .
A commit or remix prints a header
.Pq Cm commit Ar hash , Cm Author , Cm Date , indented message
followed by the unified diff against its first parent (byte-identical to
.Cm kit diff Ar parent Ar commit ) ;
a tag prints its header and then the peeled target; a tree is listed as
.Ql <mode> <type> <hash>\et<name>
lines; a blob prints its raw contents. As with
.Cm log ,
the commit/tag header carries kit's signed
.Ar Identity
and 64-hex BLAKE3 ids, so those lines diverge from git's
.Ql Author: Name <email>
/ 40-hex form; the diff body, tree listing, and blob output match git.
.Fl Fl stat
prints a diffstat
.Pq as in Cm kit diff Fl Fl stat
instead of the patch for commits and remixes.
.It Cm tree
Snapshot the working directory as a tree object and print the tree hash.
Honors
.Pa .kitignore
the same way
.Cm add
does.
.It Cm ls-tree Oo Fl r Oc Oo Fl z Oc Ar tree-ish Op Ar path ...
List a tree's entries as
.Ql <mode> <type> <hash>\et<name>
(git octal modes; 64-hex BLAKE3 hash).
.Fl r
recurses (leaf blobs with full paths, no tree lines);
.Fl z
NUL-terminates records with raw paths.
.It Cm ls-files Oo Fl s Oc Oo Fl z Oc Oo Fl Fl others Oc Oo Fl Fl ignored Oc Op Fl Fl exclude-standard
List files in the index or untracked worktree files (like
.Xr git 1 Cm ls-files ) .
Default prints tracked paths, sorted.
.Fl s
adds stage info
.Pq Ql <mode> <hash> <stage>\et<path> , No stage always 0 ;
.Fl Fl others
lists untracked files instead;
.Fl Fl exclude-standard
drops
.Pa .kitignore Ns -ignored
files and
.Fl Fl ignored
shows only those
.Pq Fl Fl ignored No requires Fl Fl others ;
.Fl z
NUL-terminates records with raw paths.
.It Cm rev-parse Oo Fl Fl short Ns Op = Ns Ar N Oc Oo Fl Fl abbrev-ref Oc Oo Fl Fl show-toplevel Oc Oo Fl Fl verify Op Fl q Oc Op Ar rev ...
Resolve revisions to object ids.
.Ar rev Ns : Ns Ar path
names the object at
.Ar path
in the tree of
.Ar rev .
.Fl Fl short
abbreviates (default 7, a BLAKE3 prefix);
.Fl Fl abbrev-ref Cm HEAD
prints the current branch;
.Fl Fl show-toplevel
prints the repository root.
.Fl Fl verify
takes exactly one revision, prints its full id and exits non-zero with nothing
on stdout when the revision does not resolve to an object in the store.
.Fl q
also drops the message.
.It Cm rev-list Oo Fl Fl count Oc Oo Fl n Ar N | Fl Fl max-count Ns = Ns Ar N Oc Ar rev | Ar A Ns .. Ns Ar B | Ar A Ns ... Ns Ar B
List the commit ids reachable from
.Ar rev
in reverse-chronological order, one per line. A range is read as in
.Cm log .
.Fl n
and
.Fl Fl max-count
keep the first
.Ar N
commits, and
.Fl Fl count
prints the number of commits left instead.
.Ar N
is a non-negative integer.
.It Cm merge-base Oo Fl Fl is-ancestor Oc Ar a Ar b
Print the best common ancestor of two commits
.Pq exit 1, no output, when none .
.Fl Fl is-ancestor
tests whether
.Ar a
is an ancestor of
.Ar b ,
exiting 0 or 1 with no output; a history that cannot be read exits 65.
.It Cm show-ref Oo Fl Fl heads Oc Oo Fl Fl tags Oc Op Ar pattern ...
List refs as
.Ql <hash> <refname> ,
sorted by name.
.Fl Fl heads Ns / Ns Fl Fl tags
filter to that namespace. A
.Ar pattern
keeps a ref that it equals, or whose trailing path components it equals. The
command exits 1 with no output when nothing matches.
.It Cm for-each-ref Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Ar fmt Oc Op Ar pattern ...
Iterate refs (heads and tags), sorted by name (like
.Xr git 1 Cm for-each-ref ) .
The default line is
.Ql <objectname> <objecttype>\et<refname> ;
.Fl Fl format
substitutes
.Ql %(atom)
tokens
.Pq Cm refname , refname:short , objectname , objectname:short , objecttype .
Optional
.Ar pattern
arguments filter by ref-name prefix.
.It Cm symbolic-ref Oo Fl Fl short Oc Ar name Op Ar ref
Read or write a symbolic ref (currently only
.Cm HEAD ) .
Without
.Ar ref ,
prints the target
.Pq Ql refs/heads/main , No or Ql main No with Fl Fl short ;
errors when HEAD is detached. With
.Ar ref
(which must be under
.Pa refs/heads/ ) ,
repoints HEAD at that branch \(em the plumbing form, which does not touch the
worktree; the branch need not exist yet.
.It Cm update-ref Oo Fl d Oc Ar ref Oo Ar newvalue Oo Ar oldvalue Oc Oc
Create, update, or delete a ref under
.Pa refs/heads/
or
.Pa refs/tags/ .
.Ar newvalue
and
.Ar oldvalue
resolve through the revspec grammar. With no
.Ar oldvalue
the write is unconditional; with one it is a compare-and-swap that fails
unless the ref currently holds that value. In update mode an all-zero
.Ar oldvalue
requires the ref to be absent (git's create-only form).
.Fl d
deletes the ref, optionally checking a
.Em concrete
.Ar oldvalue
first \(em an all-zero value is rejected in delete mode, since a ref asserted
absent has nothing to delete. Deleting a branch refuses the currently
checked-out one, a kit safety divergence.
.It Cm ref list Op Fl Fl pattern Ar glob
Print every ref's full name and resolved hash, one
.Ql <refname> <hash>
line per ref, sorted by name. Covers
.Pa refs/heads/* , refs/tags/* ,
and
.Pa refs/remotes/*/* .
An empty repo prints nothing and still exits 0.
.Fl Fl pattern
filters to full ref names matching a shell glob
.Pq Ql * No spans Ql / .
.It Cm ref cat Ar name
Print the resolved hash for one ref, following
.Cm HEAD Ns 's
symbolic indirection.
.Ar name
must be a fully-qualified ref
.Pq Pa refs/heads/<b> , refs/tags/<t> , refs/remotes/<r>/<b>
or the literal
.Cm HEAD .
.It Cm commit Oo Fl a | Fl Fl all Oc Oo Fl Fl allow-empty Oc Oo Fl Fl amend | Fl Fl fixup Ar rev | Fl Fl squash Ar rev Oc Oo Fl m Ar message | Fl F Ar file Oc Op Fl Fl author Ar spec
Create a signed commit. With
.Fl a
or
.Fl Fl all ,
first stage modified tracked files and tracked deletions, but do not add
untracked files. The combined
.Fl am Ar message
form is accepted as shorthand for
.Fl a Fl m Ar message .
If neither
.Fl m
nor
.Fl F
is given,
.Nm
opens
.Ev EDITOR
on
.Pa .kit/COMMIT_EDITMSG ;
lines starting with
.Ql #
are stripped, and an empty message aborts the commit.
.Fl F
or
.Fl Fl file
reads the commit message from
.Ar file
.Pq Ql - No reads from stdin
and is mutually exclusive with
.Fl m .
.Fl Fl author Ar spec
overrides the commit author Identity for this commit.
.Fl Fl allow-empty
accepts a first commit with nothing staged, which records an empty tree. A
commit that has a parent does not need the flag: it accepts a tree equal to
its parent's.
.Pp
After a
.Cm merge Fl Fl no-commit
.Pq or any otherwise-resolved merge ,
.Cm commit
records a two-parent merge commit from the staged merge result.
.Pp
With
.Fl Fl amend ,
replace the current commit instead of adding a new one: the new commit
re-uses HEAD's parent(s) as its own parent(s), takes its tree from the
staging index, is re-signed, and the branch is moved to it. If
.Fl m
is omitted the previous commit's message is reused and no editor is
launched. The superseded commit is not deleted; it remains on disk as an
unreachable object until
.Cm gc
reclaims it.
.Pp
With
.Fl Fl fixup Ar rev ,
the subject is
.Ql fixup!\&
followed by the subject of
.Ar rev ,
for
.Cm rebase Fl Fl autosquash
to fold into
.Ar rev ;
no editor opens, and
.Fl m
or
.Fl F
text goes in the body.
.Fl Fl squash Ar rev
writes
.Ql squash!\&
instead, and opens the editor for the body when neither
.Fl m
nor
.Fl F
is given. They do not combine with each other or with
.Fl Fl amend .
.It Cm log Oo Fl Fl oneline Oc Oo Fl Fl abbrev-commit Oc Oo Fl Fl abbrev Ns = Ns Ar N Oc Oo Fl Fl format Ar json Oc Oo Fl Fl author Ar pattern Oc Oo Fl Fl grep Ar pattern Oc Oo Fl Fl since Ar date Oc Oo Fl Fl until Ar date Oc Oo Fl Fl no-merges Oc Oo Fl Fl first-parent Oc Oo Fl p | Fl Fl stat Oc Oo Fl Fl decorate Oc Oo Fl Fl graph Oc Oo Fl n Ar N Oc Oo Fl Fl follow Oc Oo Fl L Ar start , Ns Ar end : Ns Ar file Oc Oo Fl Fl all | Ar rev | Ar A Ns .. Ns Ar B | Ar A Ns ... Ns Ar B Oc Oo Oo Fl - Oc Ar path ... Oc
Show commit history. With no argument the walk starts at
.Cm HEAD ;
an optional
.Ar rev
starts it there instead, and a range
.Ar A Ns .. Ns Ar B
shows commits reachable from
.Ar B
but not
.Ar A
(an empty side means
.Cm HEAD ) .
Commits are ordered reverse-chronologically with a topological tie-break (a
parent never precedes a child) \(em git's
.Fl Fl date-order ,
which matches git's default for linear and monotonic-timestamp history. An
.Ar A Ns ... Ns Ar B
symmetric range shows commits reachable from
.Ar A
or
.Ar B
but not their common ancestors. The default format prints the full commit message body,
indented by four spaces, and renders the timestamp as a stable UTC date in the
form
.Ql YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS +0000 .
.Fl Fl oneline
condenses each commit to
.Ql <abbrev-hex> <title> .
.Fl Fl abbrev-commit
abbreviates the commit id in the default format too, and
.Fl Fl abbrev Ns = Ns Ar N
sets the abbreviation length (bare
.Fl Fl abbrev
and
.Fl Fl oneline
default to 7, clamped to the range 4 to 64). The abbreviated id is a BLAKE3
prefix, so it is longer than a Git SHA-1 prefix of the same length.
.Fl Fl format Ar json
emits one JSON object per commit on stdout, keeping the raw integer Unix
timestamp for machine consumption.
.Fl p
(or
.Fl Fl patch )
follows each commit with its unified diff against its first parent, and
.Fl Fl stat
with its diffstat. A root commit is diffed against an empty tree, and a merge
commit against its first parent, as
.Cm show
does. The two options exclude each other and
.Fl Fl format Ar json .
.Fl Fl all
starts the walk at
.Cm HEAD ,
every local branch, every remote-tracking ref and every tag, and shows each
commit once. It is a usage error together with a revision or a range.
.Fl Fl decorate
prints the refs that name a commit after its id, in parentheses:
.Cm HEAD ,
the local branches, the tags as
.Ql tag: <name> ,
then the remote-tracking refs. Ref names print with control characters
escaped. The option is a usage error together with
.Fl Fl format Ar json .
.Fl Fl graph
draws the commit graph in the left margin with
.Ql * ,
.Ql | ,
.Ql /
and
.Ql \e ,
for the default and the
.Fl Fl oneline
format. It draws an edge only to a parent that the output shows. The option is
a usage error together with
.Fl p ,
.Fl Fl stat
or
.Fl Fl format Ar json .
.Fl Fl author Ar pattern
and
.Fl Fl grep Ar pattern
filter by a plain substring match \(em
.Fl Fl author
against the short and full identity forms,
.Fl Fl grep
against the full commit message. kit identities are opaque (Ed25519 keys,
mid numbers, DID keys), not free-text names, so this is not a regex.
.Fl Fl since Ar date
and
.Fl Fl until Ar date
bound the commit timestamp; accepted forms are
.Ql @<unix-seconds> ,
.Ql now Ns / Ns Ql today Ns / Ns Ql yesterday ,
.Ql <N> <unit> ago ,
.Ql YYYY-MM-DD ,
and
.Ql YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS
(UTC).
.Fl Fl no-merges
hides merge commits from the output;
.Fl Fl first-parent
walks only first parents, so a merged side branch never enters the walk.
These filters apply before
.Fl n Ap s
limit.
.Pp
Paths after the revision keep the commits that change them, with the
default history simplification of
.Xr git 1 :
a merge that matches one parent at the paths is left out, and the walk
follows that parent.
A path names a file or a directory; globs are an error.
Without
.Fl - ,
a path must exist in the worktree and must not also name a revision.
.Fl Fl follow
takes exactly one file and continues under its old name when a commit
renames it with the same content.
.Pp
.Fl L Ar start , Ns Ar end : Ns Ar file
follows the history of a range of lines, as
.Xr git 1
does: it lists each commit that changes a line of the range and shows
the hunk of the range, following the range back through each edit.
The range takes the forms of
.Cm blame Fl L
and may repeat, for more ranges or files.
It combines with
.Fl n ,
.Fl Fl oneline ,
.Fl Fl first-parent
and
.Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json ,
which adds a
.Li hunks
list; it takes at most one revision and no paths.
Renames are not followed.
.It Cm reflog Oo Fl Fl recovery Oc Oo Ar ref Oc Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json Oc Op Fl n Ar N
Show a branch's recorded movement history, read-only. Walks the branch
tip's first-parent chain newest-first, addressing each entry
.Ar ref Ns Cm @{ Ns Ar N Ns Cm } ,
where
.Cm @{0}
is the current tip. Defaults to the branch
.Cm HEAD
points at; a detached
.Cm HEAD
needs an explicit
.Ar ref
because ancestry snapshots are keyed per-branch.
.Pp
With
.Cm --features history-mmr
inclusion proofs are checked against the canonical ancestry snapshot and its
descriptor, binding repository, branch, generation, tip, count and root.
.Cm @{ Ns Ar N Ns Cm }
indexes the reachable first-parent chain, so commits superseded by
.Cm commit --amend
or a reset are no longer listed. This command
never mutates refs, ancestry snapshots, or objects.
.Pp
.Fl Fl recovery
lists the recovery log instead: the commits that
.Cm commit --amend ,
.Cm reset ,
.Cm rebase
and
.Cm stash pop
replaced, newest first, as
.Ql <id> recovery@{N}: <op> (<branch>): <subject> .
With
.Ar ref ,
only that branch's records are listed.
The revision
.Cm recovery@{ Ns Ar N Ns Cm }
names the commit of a record in every command, so
.Ql kit reset --hard recovery@{0}
undoes the last rewrite.
.It Cm diff Oo Fl Fl staged | Fl Fl cached Oc Oo Fl Fl name-only | Fl Fl name-status | Fl Fl stat Oc Oo Fl Fl merge-base Oc Oo Fl w | Fl b Oc Oo Fl U Ar n Oc Oo Fl z Oc Oo Fl M Ns Op Ar n | Fl Fl find-renames Ns Op = Ns Ar n | Fl Fl no-renames Oc Oo Fl l Ns Ar n Oc Oo Ar rev Op Ar rev | Ar a Ns .. Ns Ar b | Ar a Ns ... Ns Ar b Oc Ar path ...
Show changes as a unified patch. With no arguments, compares the HEAD tree to
a fresh worktree snapshot.
.Fl Fl staged
.Pq alias Fl Fl cached
compares the HEAD tree to the staged index tree. A single revision compares
that revision against the worktree; two revisions, or an
.Ar a Ns .. Ns Ar b
range, diff the two resolved trees directly. A symmetric
.Ar a Ns ... Ns Ar b
range diffs the merge base of
.Ar a
and
.Ar b
against
.Ar b .
.Fl Fl merge-base
is the flag spelling of an
.Ar a Ns ... Ns Ar b
range: it diffs the merge base of the given revisions instead of the
revisions directly.
Revisions may be refs, commit
hashes, short hashes, or
.Ql HEAD~n
(a raw 64-hex tree hash also works). Remaining positional arguments are
pathspecs that limit the output.
.Fl Fl name-only
lists one changed path per line;
.Fl Fl name-status
prefixes each with an
.Ql A Ns / Ns Ql D Ns / Ns Ql M
status letter
.Pq Ql T marks a kit mode change .
.Fl Fl stat
shows a diffstat: a padded
.Ql <name> | <count> <graph>
row per file (the
.Ql +/-
graph scaled to the terminal width, honoring
.Ev COLUMNS ,
default 80) and a
.Ql N files changed, ... insertions(+), ... deletions(-)
summary; binary files render
.Ql Bin <old> -> <new> bytes .
In these summary modes special-byte paths are C-style quoted (git
.Ql core.quotePath ) ;
.Fl z
instead NUL-terminates records and emits raw paths, and is only valid with
.Fl Fl name-only
or
.Fl Fl name-status .
.Fl w
.Pq Fl Fl ignore-all-space
ignores whitespace when comparing lines, so a line differing from its
counterpart only in whitespace shows as unchanged context;
.Fl b
.Pq Fl Fl ignore-space-change
is the weaker form where differing amounts of whitespace compare equal but a
line with whitespace where the other side has none still differs.
.Fl w
wins if both are given; either way the printed lines keep their own real
bytes, only comparison changes.
.Fl U Ar n
.Pq Fl Fl unified Ns = Ns Ar n
sets the number of unchanged context lines around each hunk (default 3).
Rename detection is on by default: a deleted and an added file pair into a
rename when their contents are at least 50% similar, scored as git scores
them.
.Fl M Ns Ar n
.Pq Fl Fl find-renames Ns = Ns Ar n
sets the threshold in git's grammar
.Po
.Ql 90%
is 90%, while digits without
.Ql %
are a fraction, so
.Ql 9
is 90%
.Pc ,
and
.Fl Fl no-renames
turns detection off.
A rename prints
.Ql R<score>
in
.Fl Fl name-status ,
.Ql old => new
in
.Fl Fl stat ,
and
.Ql similarity index ,
.Ql rename from
and
.Ql rename to
lines in the patch.
When more than
.Fl l Ns Ar n
(default 1000) squared pairs would need a score, only identical-content
renames are found and a warning goes to stderr;
.Fl l Ns 0
removes the limit.
.It Cm status Oo Fl Fl porcelain Ns Oo = Ns Cm v1 | Cm v2 Oc Oc Oo Fl s | Fl Fl short Oc Oo Fl z Oc Oo Fl Fl find-renames Ns Op = Ns Ar n | Fl Fl no-renames Oc
Show the staged and working-tree changes.
.Fl s
.Pq alias Fl Fl short
is equivalent to
.Fl Fl porcelain Ns = Ns Cm v1 :
both emit machine-readable XY-code-plus-path lines on stdout. Paths with
special bytes are C-style quoted (matching git's default
.Cm core.quotePath ) .
.Fl z
.Pq which implies porcelain
NUL-terminates records and emits raw, unquoted paths.
.Fl Fl porcelain Ns = Ns Cm v2
emits git's richer per-path format \(em
.Ql 1 <XY> <sub> <mH> <mI> <mW> <hH> <hI> <path>
for tracked changes (octal modes, full object ids; kit is never a
submodule, so
.Ar sub
is always
.Ql N... ) ,
.Ql 2 <XY> ... R<score> <path> <orig>
for a staged rename, found by similarity as in
.Cm diff
.Pq Fl Fl find-renames Ns = Ns Ar n , Fl Fl no-renames ,
and
.Ql ? <path>
for untracked. Header lines
.Pq Fl Fl branch
are not emitted.
.It Cm branch Oo Fl v | Fl Fl verbose Oc Oo Fl r | Fl a Oc Oo Fl d | Fl D | Fl m | Fl u Ar upstream | Fl Fl unset-upstream | Fl Fl show-current Oc Oo Fl Fl list Oc Oo Fl Fl contains Op Ar commit Oc Oo Fl Fl no-contains Op Ar commit Oc Oo Fl Fl merged Op Ar commit Oc Oo Fl Fl no-merged Op Ar commit Oc Oo Fl Fl format Ar format Oc Op Ar name ...
List, create, rename, or delete branches. With no arguments, lists
branches as
.Ql <marker> <name>
with no commit id, like
.Xr git 1 Cm branch ;
.Ql * Ns \&
marks the current branch.
.Fl v
.Pq Fl Fl verbose
adds the abbreviated tip id and commit subject (a BLAKE3 prefix, not a
40-hex SHA-1 prefix). With one
.Ar name ,
creates a branch at HEAD.
.Fl d
deletes a branch (refuses the current branch);
.Fl D
force-deletes. Both error on an absent branch (like
.Xr git 1 Cm branch Fl D ) ,
and both still refuse the current branch.
.Fl m Oo Ar old Oc Ar new
renames a branch \(em the current branch when
.Ar old
is omitted \(em and moves HEAD if the renamed branch is checked out.
.Fl u Ar remote Ns / Ns Ar branch Op Ar name
.Pq Fl Fl set-upstream-to
records the upstream of the branch
.Ar name ,
or of the current branch, in the repository config; the remote-tracking ref
must exist.
.Fl Fl unset-upstream Op Ar name
removes it. Neither contacts a remote.
.Fl r
lists the remote-tracking branches as
.Ql <remote>/<branch> ,
and
.Fl a
lists the local branches and then the remote-tracking branches as
.Ql remotes/<remote>/<branch> .
.Fl Fl show-current
prints the name of the current branch, and nothing on a detached HEAD.
.Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json
lists as JSONL.
.Pp
The listing accepts ancestry filters (like
.Xr git 1 Cm branch ) :
.Fl Fl contains Op Ar commit
keeps only branches whose tip has
.Ar commit
as an ancestor, and
.Fl Fl no-contains Op Ar commit
inverts that;
.Fl Fl merged Op Ar commit
keeps branches already merged into
.Ar commit
and
.Fl Fl no-merged Op Ar commit
keeps those not yet merged, both defaulting
.Ar commit
to
.Cm HEAD .
.Fl Fl list
is an explicit list selector.
.It Cm checkout Oo Fl f Oc Ar branch
Switch HEAD to a branch and restore files from its tree.
.Fl f
discards the local changes that would block the switch, as
.Xr git 1 Cm checkout Fl f
does. Untracked paths that stand in the way of a path of the target, a file or
a whole directory, are replaced; other untracked files are kept.
Prints
.Sy Switched to branch Ar n
or
.Sy Already on Ar n
for a no-op switch.
.It Cm checkout Oo Fl f Oc Li -- Ar path ...
Restore the paths from the index. This runs
.Cm restore
.Op Fl f
.Ar path ... ,
so it refuses a file with unstaged edits unless
.Fl f
is given.
.It Cm checkout Oo Fl f Oc Ar rev Li -- Ar path ...
Restore the paths from
.Ar rev
into the index and the worktree. This runs
.Cm restore
.Op Fl f
.Fl Fl source Ar rev
.Fl Fl staged
.Fl Fl worktree
.Ar path ... ,
so it refuses a file with unstaged edits unless
.Fl f
is given.
.It Cm checkout Oo Fl b | Fl B Oc Ar new Op Ar start
Create
.Pq Fl b , or create-or-reset Fl B
a branch at
.Ar start
.Pq default HEAD
and switch to it.
.It Cm switch Ar branch | - | @{-1}
Switch branches (git
.Cm switch ) .
A thin front-end over
.Cm checkout
with the same clobber guard and output.
Use
.Cm switch - , checkout - ,
or
.Cm switch @{-1}
to return to the previous HEAD, detached if it was detached.
Only one previous HEAD is stored, atomically, in the per-worktree
.Pa PREVIOUS_HEAD
file. Older selections are not supported.
.It Cm switch Oo Fl c | Fl C Oc Ar new Op Ar start
Create
.Pq Fl c , or create-or-reset Fl C
a branch and switch to it.
.It Cm tag Oo Fl a | Fl s Oc Oo Fl m Ar msg Oc Oo Fl Fl author Ar spec Oc Oo Ar name Oc Op Ar commit
List, create, or delete tags. With no arguments, prints the list of
tags, marking each annotated or signed tag. With a
.Ar name
argument and no flags, creates a lightweight tag pointing straight at
.Ar commit
.Pq default HEAD .
.Fl a
creates an annotated tag object (name, tagger identity, message,
timestamp);
.Fl s
creates a signed annotated tag whose 64-byte field is an Ed25519
signature over the canonical tag bytes under the distinct
.Sy kit.tag
domain.
.Fl m
supplies the message; without it
.Ev EDITOR
is launched.
.Fl Fl author Ar spec
overrides the tagger Identity for an annotated or signed tag.
.Fl d
deletes the named tag. Verify a signed tag with
.Cm kit verify Ar name .
.It Cm sparse-checkout Cm set | Cm list | Cm disable | Cm reapply Op Ar pattern ...
Manage the sparse-checkout pattern set at
.Pa .kit/sparse-checkout .
.Cm set
overwrites the pattern list,
.Cm list
prints the current patterns,
.Cm disable
clears them, and
.Cm reapply
re-materialises the working tree from the current patterns.
.It Cm merge Oo Fl Fl no-commit Oc Oo Fl m Ar msg Oc Oo Fl s Ar strategy Oc Oo Fl X Ar option Oc Ar branch | Fl Fl continue | Fl Fl abort
Merge
.Ar branch
into HEAD. Fast-forwards when possible.
.Fl Fl no-commit
stages the merge result but stops before committing; finish with
.Cm kit commit
or
.Cm kit merge Fl Fl continue
.Pq fast-forwards are unaffected by this flag .
.Fl m
or
.Fl Fl message
overrides the merge commit message.
.Fl X Cm ours
and
.Fl X Cm theirs
.Pq Fl Fl strategy-option
resolve each conflict in a text file to that side's lines, and a conflict in
a binary or large file to that side's whole file; changes that do not
conflict still merge from both sides.
A delete/modify conflict, a file against a directory and two different modes
still stop the merge.
.Fl s Cm ours
records a merge commit with the tree of HEAD and never fast-forwards;
.Fl s Cm ort
and
.Fl s Cm recursive
name the default merge.
After a conflict, resolve and
.Cm add
the paths, then
.Fl Fl continue ,
or
.Fl Fl abort
to restore HEAD.
.It Cm rebase Oo Fl i Oc Oo Fl Fl autosquash | Fl Fl no-autosquash Oc Oo Fl Fl update-refs | Fl Fl no-update-refs Oc Oo Fl X Ar option Oc Oo Fl x Ar cmd Oc Oo Fl Fl onto Ar newbase Oc Ar branch Op Ar branch
Replay commits from HEAD onto
.Ar branch .
With
.Fl Fl onto
the arguments are
.Ar upstream
and an optional
.Ar branch ,
and the command replays the commits of
.Ar upstream Ns .. Ns Ar branch
onto
.Ar newbase .
With
.Fl i
.Pq Fl Fl interactive
the todo list opens in
.Ev $EDITOR
first: reorder lines to reorder commits, delete a line (or mark it
.Cm drop )
to drop a commit, mark a line
.Cm reword
to edit that commit's message during replay, or fold a commit into the
previous one with
.Cm squash
(combine messages) or
.Cm fixup
(keep the previous message). A
.Cm squash Ns / Ns Cm fixup
cannot be the first line and is rejected before any mutation. Removing every
line resets the branch to the base.
.Cm edit
replays the commit and stops: amend it with
.Cm kit commit Fl Fl amend
or add commits, then run
.Cm kit rebase Fl Fl continue .
.Cm exec Ar command
runs the rest of the line with
.Ql sh -c
in the worktree root, its output on standard error; a command that fails
stops the rebase after its line, and
.Fl Fl continue
goes on.
.Fl x Ar cmd
.Pq Fl Fl exec
adds an
.Cm exec Ar cmd
line after each commit of the plan, or after the last commit of a squash or
fixup chain; it may repeat.
.Fl Fl autosquash
moves each commit whose subject starts with
.Ql fixup!\&
or
.Ql squash!\&
to just after the earlier commit it names, by subject, by id prefix of 4 or
more hex digits, or by subject prefix, and marks it
.Cm fixup
or
.Cm squash ;
a commit with no target in the range stays a
.Cm pick .
With
.Fl i
the todo list opens arranged; without it the arranged plan runs directly.
Setting
.Cm rebase.autosquash
to
.Cm true
turns it on for
.Fl i ,
and
.Fl Fl no-autosquash
turns it off.
.Fl Fl update-refs
moves every other local branch that points at a replayed commit to that
commit's replacement when the rebase finishes; with
.Fl i
the todo list shows an
.Cm update-ref Ar refs/heads/branch
line for each, which can be deleted or typed. A branch checked out in another
worktree is left out with a warning. If a branch moved during the rebase, no
branch moves and the rebase stays in progress;
.Fl Fl abort
moves no branch. Each moved branch's old tip goes to the recovery log.
Setting
.Cm rebase.updateRefs
to
.Cm true
turns it on, and
.Fl Fl no-update-refs
turns it off.
.Fl X Cm ours
and
.Fl X Cm theirs
resolve conflicts as in
.Cm merge ;
as in
.Xr git 1 ,
.Cm ours
is the base being rebased onto and
.Cm theirs
the replayed commit, and a paused rebase keeps the option.
Accepts
.Fl Fl continue ,
.Fl Fl skip ,
and
.Fl Fl abort
after a conflict.
.It Cm push Oo Fl Fl dry-run Oc Oo Fl Fl tags Oc Op Ar remote Op Ar src Ns : Ns Ar dst | Ar branch | Ar tag
.It Cm push Oo Fl Fl dry-run Oc Fl Fl delete Ar remote Ar branch | Ar tag
Push HEAD's commit chain to the configured remote. With
.Ar remote
and
.Ar src Ns : Ns Ar dst
it pushes the local branch
.Ar src
to the remote branch
.Ar dst ;
a lone
.Ar branch
means
.Ar branch Ns : Ns Ar branch ,
as in
.Xr git 1 Cm push .
The branch need not be the current one. Wildcards, a leading
.Ql + ,
an empty
.Ar src
and refs outside
.Pa refs/heads/
are errors.
A lone
.Ar tag
pushes that tag, and
.Fl Fl tags
pushes every local tag, each with its tag map; a name that is both a
branch and a tag is an error. A remote tag with another value is refused
unless
.Fl Fl force ,
and an unsigned annotated tag is refused.
.Fl Fl delete
deletes one remote branch or tag with its packmap or tag map.
A branch is deleted only while it holds its remote-tracking ref's value,
and that ref goes too; a tag only while it holds the value just read.
A name the remote holds as both a branch and a tag needs
.Pa refs/heads/
or
.Pa refs/tags/ .
An S3 remote refuses every delete.
.It Cm pull Oo Fl Fl all Oc Oo Fl Fl rebase | Fl Fl no-ff Oc Oo Fl X Ar option Oc Oo Fl Fl tags | Fl Fl no-tags Oc Op Ar remote
.It Cm fetch Oo Fl Fl all Oc Oo Fl p | Fl Fl prune Oc Oo Fl Fl tags | Fl Fl no-tags Oc Op Ar remote
Download or download-and-merge from the remote.
Both take every remote tag after the branches unless
.Fl Fl no-tags
is given;
.Fl Fl tags
says so explicitly, and the last of the two wins.
A tag publishes only once its closure is local, its tag object carries its
name and its history verifies.
A refused tag prints
.Ql ! [rejected]
with its reason, never moves a local tag and never stops the branches; the
command then exits 1.
.Cm pull Fl Fl rebase
replays the current branch onto the fetched branch, as
.Cm rebase
does, instead of fast-forwarding.
.Cm pull Fl Fl no-ff
merges the fetched branch and always writes a merge commit, even when a
fast-forward is possible; a conflict leaves the state of
.Cm merge .
It does not combine with
.Fl Fl all
or
.Fl Fl rebase .
.Fl X Cm ours
and
.Fl X Cm theirs
resolve the conflicts of the merge or the replay as in
.Cm merge
and
.Cm rebase .
.Cm fetch Fl Fl prune
then deletes the remote-tracking refs whose branch is gone from the remote;
it deletes no local branch and no tag.
.Cm fetch
also takes a closure export, as
.Ql kit+closure:// Ns Ar dir
or the path of its
.Pa MANIFEST.mkcl ,
and writes
.Pa refs/remotes/closure/ Ns Ar branch .

.It Cm clone Oo Fl Fl sparse ... Oc Oo Fl Fl tags | Fl Fl no-tags Oc Ar url
Clone a remote into a new working directory, with its tags unless
.Fl Fl no-tags
is given.
.Ar url
may be a closure export written with
.Cm closure Cm export Fl Fl history Fl Fl branch ,
as
.Ql kit+closure:// Ns Ar dir
or the path of its
.Pa MANIFEST.mkcl .
.It Cm remote Oo Cm add | Cm set | Cm remove | Cm rename Oc Op Ar args ...
Show or configure remotes. With no subcommand, lists the configured
remotes.
.Cm add
(alias
.Cm set )
adds the flat default remote
.Pq Cm remote add Ar url
or a named remote
.Pq Cm remote add Ar name url ;
URLs MUST use the strict
.Ql kit+<scheme>://
form.
.Cm remove Ar name
.Pq alias Cm rm
deletes a named remote
.Pq the reserved Ar default No name clears the flat remote ;
.Cm rename Ar old new
.Pq alias Cm mv
renames a named remote and repoints any branch upstream tracking it.
Removing or renaming a remote never touches the user-scoped
.Ar trusted_remote_endpoint ,
which is keyed by exact URL rather than remote name.
.It Cm cherry-pick Oo Fl n | Fl Fl no-commit Oc Oo Fl m Ar parent-number Oc Oo Fl X Ar option Oc Ar hash | Fl Fl continue | Fl Fl abort
Apply a single commit from elsewhere onto HEAD.
.Fl n
or
.Fl Fl no-commit
stages the picked change in the index and worktree without creating a
commit.
.Fl m
or
.Fl Fl mainline Ar parent-number
selects which parent of a merge commit is the mainline (git semantics):
it is required when replaying a merge commit and rejected for a non-merge.
This differs from
.Xr git 1 Ns 's
.Cm commit Fl m :
git's
.Cm cherry-pick Fl m
is mainline selection, not a message override.
.Fl X Cm ours
and
.Fl X Cm theirs
resolve conflicts as in
.Cm merge .
On conflict, resolve and
.Cm add
the paths, then
.Fl Fl continue ,
or
.Fl Fl abort
to restore HEAD.
.It Cm revert Ar commit | Fl Fl continue | Fl Fl abort Oo Fl n | Fl Fl no-commit Oc Oo Fl m Ar parent-number Oc Op Fl X Ar option
Create a new commit that undoes
.Ar commit
(the inverse of cherry-pick). A normal forward commit — it does not rewrite
history, so the reverted commit stays reachable. On conflict the resolvable
workflow applies:
.Fl Fl continue
after
.Cm add Ns -ing
resolutions, or
.Fl Fl abort
to restore HEAD.
.Fl n
.Pq Fl Fl no-commit
stages the reverted tree without committing.
Reverting a merge commit needs
.Fl m
or
.Fl Fl mainline Ar parent-number ,
which undoes the merge relative to that parent, counted from 1; a number out
of range, and
.Fl m
on a commit that is not a merge, are errors.
.Fl X Cm ours
and
.Fl X Cm theirs
resolve conflicts as in
.Cm merge .
.It Cm bisect Cm start | Cm good | Cm bad | Cm reset
Binary-search for the commit that introduced a regression.
.It Cm rerere Oo Cm status | Cm diff | Cm forget Ar path | Cm clear | Cm gc Oc
Inspect and maintain recorded conflict resolutions.
With
.Cm rerere.enabled
set to
.Cm true ,
.Cm merge ,
.Cm cherry-pick ,
.Cm revert
and
.Cm rebase
record how each text conflict was resolved when the operation continues or
its merge is committed, and write that resolution into the file the next
time the same conflict appears, with its sides in either order. The file is
left unstaged unless
.Cm rerere.autoUpdate
is
.Cm true .
.Cm status
lists the current conflicts that have an entry,
.Cm diff
shows the change from each conflict to its file now,
.Cm forget
drops the recorded resolution at
.Ar path ,
.Cm clear
deletes every entry, and
.Cm gc
deletes entries older than
.Cm gc.rerereResolved
.Pq default 60
or, with no resolution,
.Cm gc.rerereUnresolved
.Pq default 15
days. The cache lives in
.Pa .kit/rr-cache/ .
.It Cm gc Oo Fl n | Fl Fl dry-run Oc Oo Fl Fl grace-secs Ar secs Oc
Reclaim unreachable objects (mark-and-sweep). Keeps everything reachable from
the retention roots (refs, stash, in-progress operations, attestations, and the
recovery log), expires stale recovery entries, then deletes unreachable objects
older than the grace window
.Pq default 14 days .
.Fl n
.Pq Fl Fl dry-run
previews without deleting, and reports packed garbage.
Local packs are handled too: gc retires packs that are wholly dead past the
grace window, rewrites packs that hold a quarter or more garbage, merges small
packs and retires duplicate packs, copying what must survive into new packs
before it retires the old ones.
A damaged pack or a failed copy is kept and
reported as a warning on standard error, and so is a retirement that stopped
because a fetch, push or add held the store lock; the exit code does not
change.
.Fl Fl grace-secs Ar 0
prunes every unreachable object, but bypasses the grace window — that window is
gc's concurrency-safety net (some root publishers such as
.Cm tag
and
.Cm fetch
write an object before publishing its ref), so
.Fl Fl grace-secs Ar 0
must be run only when no other kit process is operating on the repo (gc prints
a warning). Fails closed: a corrupt root or unreadable ref aborts with nothing
removed.
.It Cm worktree Oo Cm add Ar path Op Ar commit-ish | Cm list Op Fl Fl porcelain | Cm remove Oo Fl Fl force Oc Ar path | Cm prune Op Fl Fl dry-run | Cm move Oo Fl Fl force Oc Ar path Ar new-path | Cm lock Oo Fl Fl reason Ar text Oc Ar path | Cm unlock Ar path | Cm repair Op Ar path ... Oc
Manage linked working trees.
Linked trees share the one object store and the shared refs; each tree
keeps its own
.Pa HEAD ,
index, in-progress-operation state, and stash (the stash is
deliberately per-tree, unlike
.Xr git 1 ) .
.Cm add
with no
.Ar commit-ish
creates a new branch named after the path's basename; a branch argument
checks it out, any other revision detaches. A branch can be checked out
in at most one tree —
.Cm worktree add , checkout , switch , branch Fl d ,
and
.Cm branch Fl m
refuse when another tree holds it.
.Cm remove
refuses to delete local changes, untracked files, or an in-progress
operation without
.Fl Fl force ,
and never removes the main tree.
.Cm prune
deletes registry entries whose tree is gone.
.Cm lock
marks a tree so that
.Cm remove ,
.Cm prune
and
.Cm move
leave it alone; two
.Fl Fl force
flags override the lock of
.Cm remove
and
.Cm move .
.Cm move
renames a tree to a path that does not exist and updates its links.
.Cm repair
fixes the links of a tree that was moved by hand. On-disk format:
.Pa docs/specs/SPEC-WORKTREE.md .
.It Cm stash Oo Cm save | Cm push Fl m Ar msg | Cm list | Cm pop Oo Fl Fl index Oc Op Ar ref | Cm apply Oo Fl Fl index Oc Op Ar ref | Cm drop Op Ar ref | Cm clear | Cm show Op Ar ref Oc
.Cm stash push Fl u
or
.Fl Fl include-untracked
includes non-ignored untracked files (also included by default).
.Fl a
and
.Fl Fl all
are not supported.
.Cm stash push Op Fl Fl Ar path ...
saves only matching files or directory prefixes relative to the current directory.
Other worktree files and staged entries stay unchanged.
Pop and apply refuse existing untracked destinations before writing files.

Save or restore a snapshot of working-tree changes.
.Cm pop
applies an entry and removes it;
.Cm apply
applies an entry but leaves it on the stack. Both run the
destructive-restore guard so uncommitted edits on unrelated paths are
never clobbered.
.Fl Fl index
on
.Cm pop Ns / Ns Cm apply
also restores the staged state recorded when the stash was created (like
.Xr git 1
.Cm stash pop Fl Fl index ) ,
not just the worktree changes.
.Cm save
records that staged snapshot as the stash commit's second parent.
Its local wrapper also records scope: one byte, 0 for a full stash or 1
for selected paths. Full stashes retain the whole-worktree dirty guard;
selected stashes check only saved paths.
Entries without this marker cannot be applied; there is no compatibility reader.
.Cm clear
removes every stash entry.
.Cm pop , Cm apply , Cm drop ,
and
.Cm show
take an entry selector
.Ar ref
.Pq default 0
that may be either a bare index
.Ar N
or the
.Cm stash@{ Ns Ar N Ns Cm }
form.
.It Cm apply Oo Fl Fl index | Fl Fl cached Oc Oo Fl R Oc Oo Fl Fl check | Fl Fl stat Oc Ar patch ...
Apply unified diffs, as
.Cm kit diff
or git writes them, to the worktree;
.Fl Fl cached
applies to the index alone and
.Fl Fl index
to both, which must agree.
A hunk applies only where its old lines match exactly, at the line its
header names or the nearest one; kit never applies with fuzz.
Either every file patch applies or nothing changes: a patch that does not
apply exits 1.
.Fl R
reverses the patch,
.Fl Fl check
writes nothing, and
.Fl Fl stat
prints a diffstat.
A malformed or binary patch, a copy, a symlink and a path outside the
worktree exit 65.
.It Cm am Ar mbox ... | Fl Fl continue | Fl Fl skip | Fl Fl abort
Apply each mail of a mailbox, as
.Cm git format-patch
writes it, as a signed commit on the current branch, with the
.Sy From:
of the mail as the author.
When a patch does not apply, kit changes nothing, keeps the mails in
.Pa .kit/am/
and exits 1.
.Fl Fl continue
commits the staged result,
.Fl Fl skip
drops the mail, and
.Fl Fl abort
returns to where
.Cm am
started.
.It Cm shortlog Oo Fl s Oc Oo Fl n Oc Oo Fl e Oc Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json Oc Oo Ar rev | Ar range Oc Oo Oo Fl Fl Oc Ar path ... Oc
Group the commits that
.Cm kit log
shows by author, each with its subjects oldest first.
.Fl n
sorts by count,
.Fl s
prints only the counts, and
.Fl e
keeps the email, or the key id of a key author.
With no revision and standard input that is not a terminal, kit reads
.Cm kit log
output from it.
.It Cm archive Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm tar | Ns Cm tar.gz Oc Oo Fl Fl prefix Ns = Ns Ar dir/ Oc Oo Fl o Ar file Oc Ar tree-ish Op Ar path ...
Write the tree of
.Ar tree-ish
as a tar archive, reading only the tree.
The format comes from
.Fl Fl format ,
else from the extension of
.Fl o
.Pq Pa .tar , .tar.gz , .tgz ,
else tar.
The bytes depend only on the tree: entries in tree order, uid and gid 0,
the commit's time as mtime, modes 0644, 0755 and symlinks, and a pax
global header holding the commit id.
.Fl Fl prefix
is put before every path, and each
.Ar path
keeps only that file or directory.
Without
.Fl o
the archive goes to standard output, which must not be a terminal.
.It Cm range-diff Oo Fl Fl creation-factor Ns = Ns Ar n Oc Oo Fl Fl no-dual-color Oc Ar old-base Ns .. Ns Ar old-tip Ar new-base Ns .. Ns Ar new-tip
Show how each commit of a series changed between two versions of it,
such as a branch before and after a rebase.
.Ar old Ns ... Ns Ar new
takes the merge base as both bases, and
.Ar base old-tip new-tip
takes one base for both.
kit pairs the commits by the assignment of least total cost, as git does,
and marks each line
.Sq =
for the same patch,
.Sq \&!
for a changed one, followed by the diff of the two patches,
.Sq <
for a dropped commit and
.Sq >
for an added one.
Leaving a commit unpaired costs
.Ar n Ns %
.Pq default 60
of the size of its diff.
Each side holds at most 1,000 commits.
.It Cm describe Oo Fl Fl tags Oc Oo Fl Fl always Oc Oo Fl Fl abbrev Ns = Ns Ar n Oc Oo Fl Fl long Oc Oo Fl Fl dirty Ns Oo = Ns Ar mark Oc Oc Oo Fl Fl match Ar glob Oc Oo Fl Fl exclude Ar glob Oc Op Ar rev
Name
.Ar rev
.Pq default Cm HEAD
after the nearest annotated tag that reaches it, or any tag with
.Fl Fl tags ,
as
.Ar tag Ns - Ns Ar n Ns -k Ns Ar id ,
where
.Ar n
counts the commits that
.Ar rev
reaches and the tag does not. kit writes
.Sq k
where git writes
.Sq g .
A tag on
.Ar rev
prints alone unless
.Fl Fl long .
With no tag,
.Fl Fl always
prints the short id; otherwise the exit status is 1.
.Fl Fl dirty
appends a mark when a tracked file differs from
.Cm HEAD .
.It Cm grep Oo Fl i Oc Oo Fl w Oc Oo Fl v Oc Oo Fl n Oc Oo Fl l | Fl c | Fl q Oc Oo Fl F | Fl E | Fl G Oc Oo Fl e Ar pattern Oc Ar ... Oo Ar pattern Oc Oo Fl Fl cached | Ar rev ... Oc Oo Oo Fl Fl Oc Ar path ... Oc
Print the lines of tracked files that match a pattern, as
.Ar path : Ns Ar line ,
or with
.Fl n
as
.Ar path : Ns Ar number : Ns Ar line .
It searches the tracked files in the worktree, the staged content with
.Fl Fl cached ,
or the tree of each
.Ar rev ,
whose name goes before each path. Untracked files and symlinks are never
searched.
.Fl i
ignores case,
.Fl w
keeps whole words,
.Fl v
selects lines that do not match,
.Fl l
prints the paths,
.Fl c
prints a count for each file and
.Fl q
prints nothing. Patterns use the syntax of Rust's
.Sy regex
crate;
.Fl F
takes them literally. A binary file prints
.Dq Binary file Ar path Li matches .
The exit status is 0 when a line was selected and 1 when none was.
.It Cm blame Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json Oc Op Fl w Op Fl M Op Fl C Oo Fl Fl ignore-rev Ar rev Oc Oo Fl Fl ignore-rev-precise Oc Op Fl Fl first-parent Op Fl Fl reverse Oo Fl L Ar start , end Oc Op Ar rev Ar file
Line-level attribution of
.Ar file
as of
.Ar rev
.Pq a ref, hash, or Cm HEAD~2 Ns -style spec; default Cm HEAD .
.Fl w
ignores whitespace when matching lines across revisions
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame Fl w , ignoring all whitespace ,
so a whitespace-only edit does not reattribute the line; output still
shows the file's current bytes.
.Fl M
detects lines moved within the file and
.Fl C
lines copied from other files
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame Fl M Ns / Ns Fl C ; Fl C No implies Fl M ,
and repeating
.Fl C
widens the search from files changed in the commit to every file in the
parent. Detection is block-based: the longest contiguous block above git's
default thresholds (20 alphanumeric characters for
.Fl M ,
40 for
.Fl C )
is credited to its origin, so a moved block beside new lines is still split
out and, with
.Fl w ,
a block copied with a whitespace change is still detected. git's inline
numeric forms
.Pq Fl M Ns Ar num , Fl C Ns Ar num
are not exposed.
.Fl Fl ignore-rev Ar rev
.Pq repeatable
and
.Fl Fl ignore-revs-file Ar file
skip
.Dq noise
commits during attribution
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame Fl Fl ignore-rev :
a line that would be credited to an ignored commit falls through to the
commit that previously changed it, while a line the commit genuinely
inserted stays put.
.Fl Fl ignore-rev
accepts any revision; the file holds one full hex object name per line,
skipping blank lines and
.Sq #
comments.
.Fl Fl ignore-rev-precise
.Pq kit-only; requires Fl Fl ignore-rev No or Fl Fl ignore-revs-file
refines that fall-through with content matching instead of git's
positional per-hunk guess: git pairs a fallen-through line with whatever
line sits at the same offset in the hunk, purely by position; kit hashes
line content, so it searches the parent's whole file for the line's true
surviving origin, overriding the positional guess only when content
evidence disagrees with it. The default
.Fl Fl ignore-rev
fall-through is unaffected and stays byte-identical to git.
.Fl L
restricts output to a line range:
.Ar start , end ,
.Ar start , Ns Cm + Ns Ar n
.Pq n lines forward from start ,
.Ar start , Ns Cm - Ns Ar n
.Pq n lines back, ending at start ,
.Ar start ,
.Pq to end of file ,
.Cm \&, Ns Ar end
.Pq from start of file ,
or a bare
.Ar start
.Pq to end of file .
Bounds are 1-based and inclusive; an inverted range is swapped and an
over-long end is clamped to the last line, matching
.Xr git 1 Cm blame .
Blame is merge-aware by default
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame :
at a merge a line is credited to whichever parent's side wrote it, so a line
merged in from a side branch is attributed to its authoring commit rather
than the merge.
.Fl Fl first-parent
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame Fl Fl first-parent
follows only each commit's first parent, crediting such a line to the merge
commit instead.
.Fl Fl reverse Ar start Ns Cm .. Ns Ar end
walks history forward instead of backward
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame Fl Fl reverse :
it blames the
.Ar start
version of the file and attributes each line to the last commit in the
range in which it still existed.
.Ar start Ns Cm ..
defaults
.Ar end
to
.Cm HEAD ;
an explicit
.Ar start
is required.
A line that never survives a step stays on
.Ar start
.Pq Xr git 1 prints such lines with a leading Ql ^ ; No kit's tab format has none .
.Fl w
and
.Fl L
compose with
.Fl Fl reverse .
The default output is
.Ql <short12>\et<line_num>\et<text>
per line;
.Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json
instead emits JSONL with
.Cm hash , line_num , author , timestamp ,
and
.Cm text
keys.
.It Cm prove Ar rev Op Ar path Oo Fl Fl chunk Ar N | Fl Fl range Ar OFFSET:LEN Oc Oo Fl o Ar file Oc Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json Oc
Build a disclosure bundle proving that
.Ar path
(omitted: the root tree), a
.Fl Fl chunk
of a large file, or a
.Fl Fl range Ar OFFSET:LEN
byte range belongs to
.Ar rev .
.Fl Fl chunk
and
.Fl Fl range
are mutually exclusive.
Writes the bundle to
.Fl o Ar file
or to stdout when stdout is not a TTY.
.Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json
instead emits
.Cm commit_id , path , selector , bundle_bytes , bundle_blake3 ,
and
.Cm output
keys.
.It Cm verify Ar rev
Verify the signature on a commit, remix, or signed tag.
.Ar rev
is an object hash, a branch or tag name, or
.Cm HEAD ;
a tag name resolves to its annotated-tag object when one exists.
.It Cm verify-proof Ar commit-id Ar bundle Op Fl Fl expect-path Ar path Op Fl Fl trusted Oo Fl Fl trust-roots Ar path Oc Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json Oc Oo Fl Fl payload-out Ar file Oc
Verify a disclosure bundle against a trusted 64-hex commit id.
.Ar commit-id
is not resolved as a revision.
.Ar bundle
is a file or
.Cm -
for stdin.
.Fl Fl expect-path
compares the authenticated path (SPEC-DISCLOSURE: the caller compares
the returned path) and fails on mismatch.
.Fl Fl trusted
and
.Fl Fl trust-roots
reuse
.Cm verify Ns 's
trust-roots flow against the disclosed signer.
.Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json
emits the disclosure result as JSON.
.Fl Fl payload-out Ar file
writes the verified payload bytes to
.Ar file .
.It Cm closure Cm export Ar rev Op Fl Fl history Oo Fl Fl branch Ar name Oc Oo Fl Fl since Ar rev Oc Oo Fl o | Fl Fl output Ar dir Oc Op Fl Fl force Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json Oc
Export a commit's object-set closure as
.Pa MANIFEST.mkcl
plus raw-only v1 packs (default snapshot;
.Fl Fl history
for full ancestry). Distinct from
.Cm git Cm export
(the git-bridge mirror). Default output directory is
.Pa ./<short id>.closure/ ;
.Fl o
or
.Fl Fl output Ar dir
overrides it, and
.Fl Fl force
allows writing into a non-empty directory.
.Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json
emits
.Cm root , mode , packs , objects ,
and
.Cm manifest
keys, plus
.Cm branch
and
.Cm since
when given.
.Fl Fl branch Ar name
records the branch of the root, a commit, so
.Cm clone
and
.Cm fetch
read the directory as a
.Ql kit+closure://
remote with that branch.
.Fl Fl since Ar rev
(history mode only, repeatable) leaves out every object
.Ar rev
reaches, for a receiver that holds it.
Either flag writes manifest version 2.
.It Cm closure Cm verify Ar commit-id Oo Fl Fl from Ar dir Oc Op Fl Fl history Op Fl Fl show-unreferenced Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json Oc
With
.Fl Fl from Ar dir ,
verify exported packs against a trusted 64-hex id.
Without
.Fl Fl from ,
check the local store
(reading only reachable objects by default);
.Ar commit-id
may then be a revision, and a corrupt on-disk object is reported under
.Cm corrupt
rather than a hard read failure.
.Fl Fl history
selects history vs snapshot mode when checking the local store.
The
.Cm unreferenced
list is checked only when
.Fl Fl show-unreferenced
is passed, which enumerates every local object.
.Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json
emits the closure report as JSON.
With
.Fl Fl from ,
the objects an incremental export leaves out count as present when the
local repository holds them.
.It Cm closure Cm repair-indexes
Rebuild damaged local pack indexes beside verified packs.
Run it from any
worktree, even if no revision resolves.
A pack that fails verification is kept; fetch again from the remote that
supplied it.
.It Cm attest Oo Fl Fl commit Ar hash Oc Op Fl Fl algorithm Ar alg Op Fl Fl signer Ar kind Op Fl Fl predicate-type Ar uri Op Fl Fl predicate-file Ar path
Produce a signed DSSE attestation for the given commit (defaults to
HEAD). Multiple
.Fl Fl additional-signer
specs may be supplied to co-sign the attestation. The resulting
envelope is attached to the commit's attestation set.
.Fl Fl external-signer-arg Ar V
.Pq repeatable
passes an argument to an external signer; if any instance is supplied,
the full list
.Em replaces
.Cm attest.external_signer_args
from config rather than appending to it.
.It Cm verify-attest Oo Fl Fl commit Ar hash Oc Op Fl Fl trust-roots Ar path Op Fl Fl algorithm Ar filter
Verify every attestation attached to the given commit. Returns success
only if every attached envelope verifies.
.Fl Fl algorithm
filters which signature lines are
.Em displayed
to the named algorithm; it does NOT change the pass/fail criterion — every
envelope must still verify regardless of the filter, so it cannot be used to
require that a specific algorithm signed.
.It Cm key Cm generate | Cm list | Cm import | Cm export | Cm delete
Manage user-scoped keystore keys backed by the platform keystore
(Keychain on macOS, libsecret/systemd-creds/YubiKey on Linux). Unlike
.Cm keygen
(which writes to a repo-local file), these keys persist across repos.
.It Cm grant Cm create | Cm add | Cm list | Cm revoke
Issue, import, list and revoke write and read grants (SPEC-WRITE-GRANTS).
.Cm grant create
signs a grant with the owner key
.Pq Cm ed25519 No or a software-keystore Cm secp256k1-eip191 key ,
or imports a wallet or WebAuthn signature over a statement printed with
.Fl Fl print-statement ;
.Cm grant add Oo Fl Fl remote Ar remote | Fl Fl offline Oc
verifies a grant header and adds it to the user grant store under
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kit/grants/ ,
then makes one short attempt to compare the grant's epoch with the trusted (or
named) remote's and only warns if that fails
.Pq Fl Fl offline No skips it ;
.Cm grant list Op Fl Fl check
lists it;
.Cm grant revoke Ar remote
is
.Cm epoch bump
by one and lists the local grants it invalidates. Among the grants valid for a
request the higher epoch wins, then the most specific scope, then the latest
expiry. A grant pre-issued for epoch e+1 outranks a live epoch-e grant until the
owner raises the epoch. A write-only grant never implies read on a private
repository. Each signed read may prompt for a signature when the key needs a
touch or a passphrase. A server without indexed mode applies an update to an
existing ref only under the
.Cm f
flag, so issue grants for pushes that move a branch as
.Cm cuf ,
not
.Cm cu .
This store holds client-side grants only; it does not register grants on a server.
.It Cm epoch Cm show | Cm bump
.Cm epoch show Ar remote
prints the grant epoch a remote stores for a namespace.
.Cm epoch bump Ar remote Op Fl Fl by Ar n
signs one epoch statement and re-sends the same bytes while the server answers
.Sy unavailable
with
.Sy Retry-After ,
up to
.Fl Fl timeout .
.It Cm visibility Cm set Ar remote Cm public | Cm private Op Fl Fl statement
Switch a repository between public and private, with a signed request or, with
.Fl Fl statement ,
an owner-signed statement.
.It Cm keygen Oo Fl Fl algorithm Cm ed25519 | Cm secp256k1 | Cm p256 Oc Oo Fl Fl force Oc Op Fl Fl print-pubkey
Generate a fresh keypair.
.Fl Fl algorithm
selects the key type
.Pq default Cm ed25519 ;
the destination depends on it:
.Cm ed25519
writes the signing key to the
.Cm signing_key
path
.Pq default Pa .kit/keys/default.key ,
while
.Cm secp256k1
and
.Cm p256
write attestation keys to the
.Cm attest. Ns Ar algo Ns Cm _key_path
paths
.Pq default Pa .kit/keys/secp256k1.key No / Pa .kit/keys/p256.key .
.Fl Fl force
overwrites an existing key file at the target path; and
.Fl Fl print-pubkey
emits the canonical keyid on stdout for trust-roots entries.
.It Cm config Oo Ar key Oo Ar value Oc Oc Op Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json
Read or write a config key. With no
.Ar key ,
shows every configuration value; with a
.Ar key
alone, shows that value; with a
.Ar key Ar value
pair, sets it. On the show forms,
.Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json
emits JSON instead of the default plain output. Recognized keys include
.Cm user.identity , Cm ssh.strict_host_key_checking ,
.Cm ssh.user_known_hosts_file ,
.Cm ssh.identity_file ,
.Cm rebase.autosquash ,
.Cm rebase.updateRefs ,
.Cm rerere.enabled ,
.Cm rerere.autoUpdate ,
.Cm gc.rerereResolved ,
.Cm gc.rerereUnresolved ,
and
.Cm gc.auto .
Setting
.Cm gc.auto
to
.Cm false
turns off the automatic local pack maintenance step that
.Cm commit , fetch
and
.Cm pull
run.
.It Cm serve Oo Fl Fl idle-timeout-secs Ar secs Oc Oo Fl Fl max-session-secs Ar secs Oc Ar path
Internal subcommand: act as the server end of an
.Cm kit+ssh://
transport, speaking the SSH-frame protocol on stdin and stdout.
.Fl Fl idle-timeout-secs
.Pq default 60 ; 0 disables it
ends the session, with exit status 76, after that many seconds without a
byte from the client; an upload that keeps sending never trips it.
.Fl Fl max-session-secs
.Pq default 0, off
ends the process that many seconds after it starts, whatever the client
is doing, with exit status 76.
Both accept at most 604800.
Only refs named under
.Pa refs/
are served; any other name is refused.
It has no network listener.
.It Cm mcp Op Fl Fl repository Ar path
Start a Model Context Protocol server on stdio so MCP clients can drive
local repositories: status, diff, log, show, branch, add, commit,
checkout, plus signature verification and DSSE attestation tools.
.Fl Fl repository
confines every tool call to the given path. The server runs no network
operations and never overrides
.Nm Ns 's
data-loss guards.
.It Cm git Cm export Oo Fl Fl remote-name Ar name Oc Oo Fl Fl ref Ar ref Oc Oo Fl Fl passthrough Oc Oo Fl Fl no-attest Oc Oo Fl Fl algorithm Ar alg Oc Oo Fl Fl signer Ar kind Oc Oo Fl Fl json Oc Ar dest
Deterministic one-way export of branches and tags to a git mirror per
SPEC-GIT-BRIDGE. kit-only fields ride in
.Ql kit-*
commit headers so the original signed objects are reconstructible;
untranslatable refs are skipped per-ref with a warning. Pushes use
per-ref
.Fl Fl force-with-lease
from state recorded under
.Pa .kit/git/<name>/ .
The default
.Fl Fl remote-name
is
.Ql mirror ;
.Fl Fl ref
takes full names
.Pq Ql refs/heads/... , Ql refs/tags/... .
Unless
.Fl Fl no-attest ,
each exported head gets a signed git-bridge/v1 DSSE attestation
(signer resolution as in
.Cm kit attest :
.Fl Fl algorithm / Fl Fl signer
flags, else config defaults),
published on the mirror's
.Ql refs/kit/attestations
ref. With
.Fl Fl passthrough
(fork mode, SPEC-GIT-BRIDGE \(sc14), an imported repository publishes
as a true git fork: imported objects re-emit their original sha1s and
only native commits translate, so merge bases with the upstream exist
and pull requests work. The import direction is
.Cm kit git import
(SPEC-GIT-IMPORT); there is no bidirectional sync. Requires a
.Cm git
binary on
.Ev PATH .
.It Cm git Cm import Oo Fl Fl remote-name Ar name Oc Oo Fl Fl key Ar path Oc Oo Fl Fl json Oc Ar url Op Ar dir
Import a git upstream as an importer-signed downstream fork per
SPEC-GIT-IMPORT. With
.Ar dir ,
initialise a fresh repository and check out the upstream default
branch; without, add tracking refs to the current repository. Signed
by a dedicated import key, pinned per state dir.
.It Cm git Cm fetch | Cm pull Oo Fl Fl remote-name Ar name Oc Oo Fl Fl key Ar path Oc Op Fl Fl json
Update
.Pa refs/remotes/<name>/*
and imported tags from the upstream (a locally-moved tag is never
clobbered);
.Cm pull
also fast-forwards the current branch (divergence refuses with a
.Cm kit merge
hint).
.Fl Fl key Ar path
selects the import signing key
.Pq default the pinned key file ,
and
.Fl Fl json
emits machine-readable JSON on stdout.
.It Cm git Cm verify Oo Fl Fl remote-name Ar name Oc Oo Fl Fl fork-audit Oc Oo Fl Fl ref Ar ref Oc
Audit recorded bridge state against the local store: bridge-translated
objects shallow-verify and must reconstruct to their mapped twin;
imported objects must have retained raw bytes hashing to their sha1
and a twin signed by the pinned importer key.
.Fl Fl fork-audit
additionally re-derives every tree and blob referenced by bridge
commits (SPEC-GIT-BRIDGE \(sc14.3). Exits non-zero listing each
failing object; unsigned (all-zero-signature) objects also fail,
reported as a count.
.It Cm git Cm status
Show every bridge state dir: direction, recorded endpoints, pinned
importer key, tracking and lease ref positions, staging health.
.It Cm git Cm format-patch Oo Fl o Ar dir Oc Oo Fl Fl stdout Oc Ar range
Render native commits in
.Ar range
.Pq Ql A..B , or Ql rev for Ql rev..HEAD
as
.Cm git am Ns
-able mbox patches, oldest first; merge commits are skipped.
.It Cm self Cm update Oo Fl Fl version Ar tag Oc Oo Fl Fl check Oc Oo Fl Fl allow-downgrade Oc Oo Fl Fl format Ar human|json Oc
Update this binary in place from a GitHub Release. The
downloaded archive is checked against its sha256 sidecar asset when
the release publishes one; this runs entirely in-process \(em no
.Cm cosign
or GitHub attestation API required. Only installer-managed binaries
(installed via
.Ql curl kit.storage | sh ,
which writes the
.Pa .kit-installed-tag
receipt) are updated; Homebrew and cargo installs are refused with
channel-specific guidance instead.
.Fl Fl check
only reports whether an update is available and changes nothing.
Resolving
.Ql latest
never downgrades; an explicit
.Fl Fl version
pin may only downgrade together with
.Fl Fl allow-downgrade .
Receipts
.Pq Pa .kit-installed-tag , KIT_STATE_DIR/installed-tag
are rewritten after a successful swap, in the installer's format.
There is no background update check; the command only ever acts when
invoked.
.It Cm version
Print the version to stdout as
.Ql kit <X.Y.Z> .
The top-level
.Fl Fl version
and
.Fl V
flags are aliases of this subcommand and emit the identical string. Note this
is
.Ql kit <X.Y.Z> ,
not Git's
.Ql git version <X.Y.Z>
form \(em an intentional, packaging-pinned divergence.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "CLICOLOR_FORCE"
.It Ev GIT_EDITOR , Ev EDITOR , Ev VISUAL
Editor opened by
.Cm kit commit
when neither
.Fl m
nor
.Fl F
is supplied, tried in that order. If none is set it falls back to
.Xr vi 1 ,
matching git.
.Cm kit tag -a
and
.Cm kit rebase -i
use the same editor. An editor that is set always runs, terminal or not.
When none is set and stdin is not a terminal, these commands start no
.Xr vi 1
and fail at once: pass
.Fl m
or
.Fl F ,
or set an editor.
.It Ev NO_COLOR
If set to any value (including empty),
.Nm
suppresses ANSI color output on stdout. See
.Lk https://no-color.org .
.It Ev CLICOLOR_FORCE
If set to
.Ql 1 ,
.Nm
emits ANSI color even when stdout is not a terminal.
.Ev NO_COLOR
overrides this.
.It Ev SSH_AUTH_SOCK
Standard OpenSSH agent socket; used when pushing or cloning via
.Cm kit+ssh:// .
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME , Ev XDG_DATA_HOME , Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME , Ev XDG_STATE_HOME
Locations of user-level config, keystore, cache, and state
respectively. See
.Sx FILES .
.It Ev GH_TOKEN , Ev GITHUB_TOKEN
Bearer token
.Cm kit self update
sends to the GitHub API, tried in that order. Optional for public
releases; required while the repository is private.
.It Ev KIT_STATE_DIR
Receipt state dir consulted by
.Cm kit self update
(default
.Pa ~/.local/state/kit ) ,
matching the installer's variable of the same name.
.It Ev KIT_SELF_UPDATE_API_BASE
Override the release-API base URL used by
.Cm kit self update
(default
.Lk https://api.github.com/repos/officialunofficial/kit ) .
For tests and mirrors.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.local/state/kit/"
.It Pa .kit/
Repo-local state dir (analogous to
.Pa .git/ ).
.It Pa .kit/config
Repo-local config; overrides user-level values for ordinary keys. Security-
sensitive keys
.Pq Cm signing_key , Cm user.identity , Cm signer , Cm key.* , Cm attest.* , Cm ssh.* , No and Cm trusted_remote_endpoint
are user-scoped only and ignored here.
.It Pa .kit/keys/default.key
Repo-local Ed25519 signing key.
.It Pa .kit/index
Staged-change index.
.It Pa .kit/index.lock
Lockfile held by
.Cm commit , Cm checkout , Cm merge , Cm rebase
to serialize concurrent invocations.
.It Pa .kit/COMMIT_EDITMSG
Scratch file written before
.Ev EDITOR
is invoked by
.Cm commit .
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kit/config
User-level config.
.It Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/kit/keys/
User-level keystore (optional; for a single identity across repos).
.It Pa $XDG_CACHE_HOME/kit/
User-level cache directory.
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/kit/
User-level state directory.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
follows the BSD
.Xr sysexits 3
conventions where practical:
.Bl -tag -width "76"
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
General error (catch-all).
.It 64
Command-line usage error (unknown subcommand, missing required arg).
.It 65
Input data was malformed (corrupt object, bad hex hash).
.It 66
Input file was missing or unreadable.
.It 69
Service unavailable (transport could not connect).
.It 73
Could not create output (permission denied writing a ref, etc.).
.It 75
Temporary failure; retrying is safe (5xx after retry budget,
SIGINT/SIGTERM interruption).
.It 76
Protocol error (bad URL scheme, malformed server response).
.It 77
Permission denied.
.It 78
Config error (unknown key, invalid value).
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Errors are written to stderr on a single line beginning with
.Ql error: .
When
.Ev NO_COLOR
is unset and stdout is a terminal, colored labels may be used; consult
.Ev CLICOLOR_FORCE
to override.
.Pp
.Nm
installs
.Dv SIGPIPE
as ignored, so common pipelines like
.Dl kit log | head -1
exit cleanly without a "Broken pipe" message.
.Dv SIGINT
and
.Dv SIGTERM
usually stop a local command at once.
In
.Cm push ,
.Cm pull ,
.Cm fetch ,
.Cm clone ,
.Cm log ,
.Cm reflog ,
.Cm grant ,
.Cm epoch
and
.Cm visibility
the first one sets a flag that the command honors at its next checkpoint.
.Cm merge ,
.Cm rebase ,
.Cm cherry-pick ,
.Cm revert ,
.Cm stash ,
.Cm bisect ,
.Cm worktree ,
.Cm remote
and
.Cm git pull
let the first one pass, so a multi-step operation is not cut between two
writes.
In all of them a second one stops the command at once.
While an editor runs,
.Nm
ignores
.Dv SIGINT
and
.Dv SIGQUIT
and waits for the editor to exit;
.Dv SIGTERM
still ends it.
When
.Cm checkout
or
.Cm switch
starts restoring files for a branch or detached HEAD, the first signal
lets it finish updating the worktree, HEAD, and index.
A second signal stops it immediately.
